/li><li>  $x^3 &lt; 0$ </li></ol>","choice1":"I only","choice2":"I and II only","choice3":"II only","choice4":"III only","choice5":"II and III only","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>We say that an implication has a <em>vacuous proof</em> if the antecedent is always false: </p>\n<p>\\[\n\\overbrace{{\\color{blue}P}}^{\\textrm{Always false}} \\Rightarrow \\quad Q\n\\]</p>\n<p>It does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. </p>\n<p>With that in mind, let&#39;s examine our antecedents in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Statement I is always false. $\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Predicate II, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ is always true. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Predicate III, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ is <em>not</em> always false. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is &quot;I only.&quot;</p>\n","topic":{"id":2815,"name":"Trivial and Vacuous Proofs"},"example":{"id":6951,"title":"Completing Vacuous Statement"},"group":{"id":15082,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14258395,"answer":{"id":62339684,"cread":"2026-10-16T07:54:46.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":19293},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62339728,"created":"2026-10-16T07:55:03.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":2,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":11952,"number":9,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 7:55 AM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:12","question":{"id":216056,"format":"MC","difficulty":2,"exampleId":6951,"text":"<p>Let $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Consider the following statement:</p>\n<p>$\\qquad$ If $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_,$ then $\\cos x &lt; \\dfrac12.$ </p>\n<p>Which of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?</p>\n<ol class=\"questionStatements\"><li>  $0 \\leq \\cos^2{x} \\leq 1$ </li><li>  $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} &lt; x &lt; \\pi$ </li><li>  $\\cos{\\pi}= 1$ </li></ol>","choice1":"III only","choice2":"I only","choice3":"I and II only","choice4":"II only","choice5":"II and III only","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>We say that an implication has a <em>vacuous proof</em> if the antecedent is always false:\n\\[\n\\overbrace{{\\color{blue}P}}^{\\textrm{Always false}} \\Rightarrow \\quad Q\n\\]</p>\n<p>It does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. </p>\n<p>With that in mind, let&#39;s examine our antecedents in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Predicate I, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is always true. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Predicate II, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is <em>not</em> always false. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is always false. $\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is &quot;III only.&quot;</p>\n","topic":{"id":2815,"name":"Trivial and Vacuous Proofs"},"example":{"id":6951,"title":"Completing Vacuous Statement"},"group":{"id":31942,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14258395,"answer":{"id":62339728,"cread":"2026-10-16T07:55:03.000Z","choices":null,"correct":0,"selectedChoice":2,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":11952},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyModerate","difficultySymbol":"M","rendered":true,"testMode":false}},{"id":62339761,"created":"2026-10-16T07:55:25.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":16461,"number":10,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 7:55 AM","timeElapsedFormatted":"0:16","question":{"id":216321,"format":"MC","difficulty":1,"exampleId":6951,"text":"<p>Let $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Consider the following statement:</p>\n<p>$\\qquad$ If $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_,$ then $|\\sin x| \\leq 1.$</p>\n<p>Which of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?</p>\n<ol class=\"questionStatements\"><li>  $|x| &lt;  x$ </li><li>  $\\sqrt{x-1} \\in \\mathbb R$ </li><li>  $\\sin \\pi =0$ </li></ol>","choice1":"I only","choice2":"II only","choice3":"III only","choice4":"I and II only","choice5":"II and III only","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p>We say that an implication has a <em>vacuous proof</em> if the antecedent is always false: </p>\n<p>\\[\n\\overbrace{{\\color{blue}P}}^{\\textrm{Always false}} \\Rightarrow \\quad Q\n\\]</p>\n<p>It does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. </p>\n<p>With that in mind, let&#39;s examine our antecedents in turn.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Predicate I, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is always false. $\\:{\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$ </p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Predicate II, defined for $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ is <em>not</em> always false. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>Statement III is always true. $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Therefore, the correct answer is &quot;I only.&quot;</p>\n","topic":{"id":2815,"name":"Trivial and Vacuous Proofs"},"example":{"id":6951,"title":"Completing Vacuous Statement"},"group":{"id":15082,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[],"inputAnswers":[],"body":null,"taskId":14258395,"answer":{"id":62339761,"cread":"2026-10-16T07:55:25.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":1,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":16461},"graphic":false,"difficultyClass":"difficultyEasy","difficultySymbol":"E","rendered":true,"testMode":false}}]},{"id":6948,"number":3,"title":"Proving a Trivial or Vacuous Statement","answers":[{"id":62339930,"created":"2026-10-16T07:56:39.000Z","choices":null,"correct":1,"selectedChoice":null,"freeResponse":null,"timeElapsed":66213,"number":11,"createdStr":"Fri, Oct 16th @ 7:56 AM","timeElapsedFormatted":"1:06","question":{"id":216190,"format":"PR","difficulty":1,"exampleId":6948,"text":"<p>Let $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$ Prove that if $0 \\lt x \\lt 3,$ then $x^2 - 6x + 12 \\gt 0.$</p>\n","choice1":" ","choice2":" ","choice3":" ","choice4":" ","choice5":" ","freeAnswerChoice":" ","explanation":"<p><p>We say that an implication has a <em>trivial proof</em> if the consequent is always true:\n\\[\nP \\quad \\Rightarrow \\overbrace{{\\color{blue}Q}}^{\\textrm{Always true}}\n\\]</p></p>\n<p><p>It does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false. </p></p>\n<p><p>With that in mind, we proceed as follows:</p></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The theorem can be written as the implication \n\\[\n0 \\lt x \\lt 3\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\nx^2 - 6x + 12 \\gt 0.\n\\]</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now, notice that for each $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have\n\\begin{align}\nx^2 - 6x + 12 & = (x^2 - 6x + 9) + 3\\\\[5pt]\n& = (x - 3)^2 + 3\\\\[5pt]\n& \\geq 3\\\\[5pt]\n& \\gt 0.\n\\end{align}</p>\n<p>So, we have the following fact:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The consequent $x^2 - 6x + 12 \\gt 0$ of our implication is always true.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Finally, we can make the following conclusion:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Therefore, the theorem is trivially true.</p>\n</blockquote>\n","topic":{"id":2815,"name":"Trivial and Vacuous Proofs"},"example":{"id":6948,"title":"Proving a Trivial or Vacuous Statement"},"group":{"id":31940,"calculator":"None"},"graphics":[],"selects":[{"options":["0 \\lt x \\lt 3","x^2 - 6x + 12 \\gt 0"],"margin":{"left":0,"right":0,"top":0,"bottom":0},"context":"mathJax","index":0,"id":6231,"sectionIndex":0,"answers":[{"selectedIndex":0,"correct":1}],"correct":1,"selectedIndex":0,"correctIndex":0},{"options":["x^2 - 6x + 12 \\gt 0","0 \\lt x \\lt 3"],"margin":{"left":0,"right":0,"top":0,"bottom":0},"context":"mathJax","index":1,"id":6232,"sectionIndex":0,"answers":[{"selectedIndex":0,"correct":1}],"correct":1,"selectedIndex":0,"correctIndex":0},{"options":["consequent","antecedent"],"margin":{"left":4,"right":4,"top":0,"bottom":2},"context":"text","index":2,"id":6233,"sectionIndex":1,"answers":[{"selectedIndex":0,"correct":1}],"correct"